C o m p i l a t i o n


      Irrigation Mixed Integer Problem
      CEE 6410/5410
      Ammon Wallace
      October 14, 2026
       
      This problem is taken from chapter 7 in Bishop, A Bruce; Hughes, Trevor; and McKee, Mac, "Water Resources Systems Analysis - Course Notes"
      (1999). All ECSTATIC Materials. Paper 76.
      https://digitalcommons.usu.edu/ecstatic_all/76Learning objectives.
       
      A farmer plans to develop water for irrigation. He is considering two possible sources of
      water: a gravity diversion from a possible reservoir with two alternative capacities and/or a
      pump from a lower river diversion (refer to Figure 7.3). Between the reservoir and pump
      site the river base flow increases by 2 acft/day due to groundwater drainage into the river.
      Ignore losses from the reservoir. The river flow into the reservoir and the farmer's demand
      during each of two six-month seasons of the year are given in Table 7.5. Revenue is esti-
      mated at $300 per year per acre irrigated.
       
      Season, t       River Inflow,       Qt (acft) Irrigation Demand (acft/acre)
          1               600                             1.0
          2               200                             3.0
       
      Assume that there are only two possible sizes of reservoir: a high dam that has capacity of
      700 acft or a low dam with capacity of 300 acft. The capital costs are $10,000/year and
      $6,000/year for the high and low dams, respectively (no operating cost). The pump capac-
      ity is fixed at 2.2 acft/day with a capital cost (if it is built) of $8,000/year and operating cost
      of $20/acft.
       
       
      Ammon Wallace
      ammon.wallace@usu.edu
  33   
  34  Sets
  35  loc The location of water /Res, River, Farm, Pump/
  36  source Different sources to choose from /High_dam, Low_dam, Pump/
  37  t The irrigation season /1, 2/;
  38   
  39  Parameters
  40  river_gain The river base flow increase due to groundwater  (acft per day) /2/
  41  unit_rev The revenue per acre irrigated in dollars /300/
  42  river_inflow(t) The river inflow for each season (acft) /1 600, 2 200/
  43  irr_demand(t) The irrigation demand (acft per acre) for each season /1 1.0, 2 3.0/
  44  Capacity(source) The capacity of each source (acft) and (acft per day) for pump /High_dam 700, Low_dam 300, Pump 2.2/
  45  Cost(source) The cost of each dam in dollars per year /High_dam 10000, Low_dam 6000, Pump 8000/
  46  Operating_cost_pump The operating cost of the pump in dollars per acft /20/
  47  days_in_season the number of days in a 6 month season /182.5/
  48  res_storage_init The initial res storage (ac-ft) /0/;
  49   
  50  Variables
  51  I(source) binary decision variable for each source
  52  TPROFIT total profit gained
  53  X(loc, t) How much water to send to each location for each season (ac-ft)
  54  A Number of Acres to plant;
  55   
  56  Binary Variables
  57  I(source);
  58   
  59  Positive Variables
  60  X(loc,t)
  61  S(t)
  62  A;
  63   
  64  Equations
  65  Objective The objective function
  66  Mass_balance_res(t) The mass balance constraint at the reservoir
  67  Mass_balance_pump(t) The mass balance constraint at the pump
  68  Dam_capacity(t) The dam capacity constraint
  69  Pump_capacity(t) The pump capacity constraint
  70  Mutually_exclusive_dams The constraint that only 1 dam will be decided
  71  Irrigation_demand(t) The irrigation demand from number of acres planted;
  72   
  73   
  74  Objective.. A*unit_rev - sum(source, I(source)*Cost(source)) - sum(t, X('Pump',t)*Operating_cost_pump) =E= TPROFIT;
  75  Mutually_exclusive_dams.. I('High_dam')+I('Low_dam') =L= 1;
  76  Dam_capacity(t).. X('Res',t) =L= Capacity('High_dam')*I('High_dam') + Capacity('Low_dam')*I('low_dam');
  77  Mass_balance_res(t).. X('Res',t) =E= (res_storage_init$(ord(t) eq 1)) +
  78                        (X('Res',t-1)$(ord(t) gt 1)) + river_inflow(t) - X('River', t) - X('Farm', t);
  79  Pump_capacity(t).. X('Pump',t) =L= days_in_season*capacity('Pump')*I('Pump');
  80  Mass_balance_pump(t).. X('Pump',t) =L= X('River',t) + river_gain*days_in_season;
  81  Irrigation_demand(t).. X('Farm',t) + X('Pump',t) =E= A * irr_demand(t);
  82   
  83  Model Seasons /All/;
  84   
  85  Solve Seasons using MIP maximizing TPROFIT;
  86   
  87  Display X.L, TPROFIT.L, A.L, I.L;


